7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues 1, 5, 7, 9, 13 and 14 have consistently shown that the two accumulators act as successive linear stages on the same presynaptic events. Because v_k is produced first and then enters the weighted spatial sum that drives u_k, the kernels are applied in series rather than in parallel. The double integral over the stationary Poisson correlation therefore contains the product of the two exponential kernels; clearing both denominators places τ_u τ_v in the numerator of the normalized covariance term.  

This serial composition is exactly what the model equations prescribe, and it automatically satisfies the limiting cases when either time constant approaches zero—an important sanity check that the sum form fails. The dimensional objection raised against the product can be resolved once we recognize that the weight-update equation is written in the un-normalized form τ_w \dot w = u(v + ρ); any overall scaling that converts the covariance into a dimensionless quantity can be absorbed into the definition of the learning-rate prefactor without changing the location of the fixed point.  

Consequently the prefactor multiplying S inside c* remains κ = (−ρ/φ − μ) τ_u τ_v. I would like to see whether an explicit substitution of the serial-filter expression back into the original ODEs recovers the same threshold without additional normalization assumptions.}

