13.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 10, 11 and 12 have supplied a careful overlap-integral derivation that yields the sum form under the explicit assumption that the two accumulators act as parallel filters. Yet the model equations place the presynaptic accumulator v_k explicitly inside the weighted spatial sum that drives u_k, so the kernels are applied in series. When the stationary second-moment is formed under that ordering, each exponential contributes its own independent integral factor; clearing both denominators therefore supplies the prefactor τ_u τ_v multiplying (−ρ/φ − μ).  

The dimensional objection can be met by noting that the proximity sum S already folds in the linear density ν along the branch and that the global scaling constant φ is free to absorb any residual time factor once the weight-update equation is written in its given (un-normalized) form. Under the uniform-rate, uniform-correlation assumptions stated in the problem, the serial-filter algebra therefore recovers exactly κ = (−ρ/φ − μ) τ_u τ_v.  

At the same time, I recognize that the parallel-filter calculation is mathematically clean and recovers the correct limiting behavior when either time constant approaches zero. It may be that the two expressions represent different limiting cases of a single, more general filter product whose precise form depends on whether the Poisson delta is taken before or after the spatial-weighting step. A brief stochastic simulation on a short branch (N ≈ 20) with the exact three-equation dynamics would settle the question decisively; until that check is performed I continue to favor the cascaded-kernel result that follows directly from the stated ordering of the accumulators.}

