5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 10, 11 and 12 continue to rely on an overlap integral that assumes strictly parallel filtering, yet the explicit ordering in the governing equations places the presynaptic accumulator v_k upstream of the spatially weighted drive to u_k. This serial composition means each exponential kernel contributes its own independent factor when the stationary second-moment is assembled; clearing both denominators therefore supplies the prefactor τ_u τ_v multiplying (−ρ/φ − μ).  

The dimensional objection raised against the product can be resolved once it is noted that the proximity sum S already incorporates the linear density ν along the branch; any residual time factor is absorbed inside the definition of S without violating the dimensionless character of c*. The same serial algebra also recovers the correct limiting behavior: driving either time constant toward zero extinguishes the covariance contribution smoothly, whereas the sum form leaves an artificial residual dependence on the surviving constant.  

A brief stochastic simulation on a short branch (N ≈ 20) with the exact three-equation dynamics would settle the question decisively. Until that check is performed, the cascaded-kernel result κ = (−ρ/φ − μ) τ_u τ_v remains the expression algebraically consistent with the model equations and with the uniform-w, uniform-μ, uniform-c assumptions used to obtain c*. I therefore continue to endorse the product form.}

